{"step_index":2300,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T15:30:10Z","input_tokens":127490,"cache_read_tokens":29102,"output_tokens":1697,"content":"### Data as First-Class D-Bus Objects\n\nThis is the hidden architectural core of the entire system: **data is not buried in an opaque SQL database or hidden behind custom APIs—the data itself IS a D-Bus object.**\n\n---\n\n### The Object Hierarchy on the Local Bus\n\nIn standard D-Bus architecture, everything forms a typed object tree:\n\n```\nD-BUS OBJECT TREE (org.opdbus.v1)\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n/org/opdbus/v1/plugins/<plugin_name>                 <-- The Plugin Object\n  │\n  ├── /org/opdbus/v1/plugins/<plugin>/<child_entity_1> <-- Data Entity #1 (A D-Bus Object)\n  └── /org/opdbus/v1/plugins/<plugin>/<child_entity_2> <-- Data Entity #2 (A D-Bus Object)\n```\n\nFor example, in a Title & Escrow or Memory service:\n* `/org/opdbus/v1/plugins/escrow` (The parent plugin)\n* `/org/opdbus/v1/plugins/escrow/transfers/deal_491` (The child data entity as a living D-Bus object)\n\n---\n\n### Why Treating Data as D-Bus Objects Changes Everything\n\n1. **Standard Typed Properties (`org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties`)**:\n   * Each data entity implements standard D-Bus property interfaces (`Get`, `Set`, `GetAll`).\n   * The fields of the record (balances, state, timestamps, owners) are typed D-Bus properties defined in the sealed schema.\n   * You don't need a custom database driver; any tool on the bus can inspect and read the data natively.\n\n2. **Native Reactive Signals (`PropertiesChanged`)**:\n   * When data changes, the child D-Bus object emits a standard `PropertiesChanged` signal across the local bus.\n   * The FUSE spine and the bridge listen to these signals in real time with **zero polling loops**.\n\n3. **1:1 Mapping to the FUSE Filesystem**:\n   Because D-Bus object paths use standard Unix slash-delimited hierarchies, **the FUSE filesystem mirrors the D-Bus object tree 1:1**:\n   ```\n   D-Bus Path:  /org/opdbus/v1/plugins/escrow/deal_491\n   FUSE Inode:  /run/opdbus/lungs/plugins/escrow/deal_491/state\n   ```\n   Reading the file on the FUSE side reads the D-Bus object's properties directly over the local connection.\n\n4. **How the Node Feeds Through the Door**:\n   * The **Node** (outside the bus) sends a call to the **Door (`:8090`)**.\n   * The Door checks the node's capability and maps the call directly to that child object's path on the local D-Bus:\n     `CallMethod(path=\"/org/opdbus/v1/plugins/escrow/deal_491\", interface=\"...\", method=\"Release\")`.\n   * The child D-Bus object mutates, emits its signal, and the local FUSE filesystem updates instantaneously.\n\nThe whole system—from sealed blob to D-Bus object, to FUSE file, to Door gRPC—is a **single, unified, typed object space**.","thinking":"The user's statement highlights that the plugin, and its child, represent data entities. This aligns with the understanding that data within the system is represented as D-Bus objects. Examining the D-Bus hierarchy is necessary to understand how the plugin and its child data entities interact within this framework.\n\n\nThis D-Bus model uses objects and object paths to represent plugins and their data entities, ensuring standardized interfaces for data access and modification. The FUSE mapping directly mirrors the D-Bus hierarchy as directories and files for direct object access and manipulation. Each data entity object will be introspectable, with properties, and signals for efficient data monitoring.\n\n\n"}
